    "result_text": "My change is complete and correct. Let me summarize what I found and did.\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented the soft-match \"possible duplicate\" rule on owner creation (`POST /api/owners`).\n\n**The rule:** when a new owner is *not* a hard duplicate (it already passed the existing `identityKey` check) but shares an existing owner's `lastName` (case-insensitive) and `postcode` while having a **different** telephone, the owner is still created and flagged with `possibleDuplicate: true` and `possibleDuplicateOf` set to the matching owner's id. Otherwise `possibleDuplicate: false`.\n\n**Changes made:**\n1. **`model/Owner.java`** \u2014 added persisted `possibleDuplicate` (Boolean) and `possibleDuplicateOf` (Integer) fields with getters/setters and `@Column` mappings.\n2. **`rest/controller/v1/OwnerRestControllerV1.java`** \u2014 added `assignPossibleDuplicate(owner)`, called just before `saveOwner`. It scans existing owners for a matching `lastName`+`postcode` with a differing telephone and records the earliest matching id (deterministic when several match).\n3. **`resources/openapi.yml`** \u2014 added the two read-only properties to the `Owner` schema so the generated `OwnerDto` exposes them (MapStruct maps them by name automatically).\n4. **`db/{h2,hsqldb,mysql,postgres}/schema.sql`** \u2014 added the `possible_duplicate` / `possible_duplicate_of` columns so the values round-trip through persistence.\n\n**Test results:** The new `Cp35Tests` passes. The full suite runs 48 tests with a single failure \u2014 `Cp28Tests` \u2014 which I verified is **pre-existing and unrelated to my change**:\n\n- With my new logic disabled, `Cp28` still fails, so my change didn't cause it.\n- `Cp28` expects two owners sharing only a telephone (different household) to be rejected with 409. That contradicts the currently-implemented consolidated `identityKey` rule (`telephone|email|householdId`) which `Cp03`, `Cp10`, and especially `Cp11` depend on \u2014 `Cp11` explicitly requires same-household/different-telephone owners to both be created. `Cp28` represents an older standalone telephone-duplicate rule that was superseded; it cannot be made green without breaking those other passing checkpoints.\n\nEvery test except that pre-existing, mutually-exclusive `Cp28` failure is green, including the new soft-match test.",
